  :adr/id "adr-2608272200-bots-on-the-openrouter-free-plan"
  :adr/title "Bots run on the OpenRouter free plan, and a profile that enables a provider now does something"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-27"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

Owner instruction, 2026-08-27: run the Bots on OpenRouter's free plan, matching
what had just been done for the hyakka growth bots in the superproject
(root ADR-2608272100).

## What was in the way, and was not visible

A deployment enables a provider in its profile. That is what
`profiles/itonami.edn` says it is for, in its own header: the one thing the
tenant-neutral distribution cannot ship.

**Profiles could not enable a provider.** `load-config` collected provider
overrides from the store's `config.edn` only, and then `assoc`ed the result over
the merged map:

    config (assoc (deep-merge defaults profile (dissoc overrides :providers))
                  :providers providers)

A profile's `:providers` were merged into the intermediate value by `deep-merge`
and thrown away one form later by the `assoc`. Measured against the real loader
before changing anything:

| profile contains | effect |
|---|---|
| `:bots {:mail-domain \"probe.example\"}` | applied |
| `:providers [{:id \"murakumo\" :enabled? true :reviewed? true}]` | **`enabled? false reviewed? false`** |

No error, no warning. A profile that turned a provider on read as correct and
did nothing — and the two facts came out of the same file in the same load.

The fix is `overlay-providers`, which applies layers in order —
defaults <- profile <- config.edn — so the store's own file still has the last
word. It is a public function so the test can reach it: a JVM cannot set
`CLOUD_ITONAMI_PROFILE` for itself, which is the same limitation the config
test namespace already states about `data-dir`, and testing through the env var
would have meant not testing it.

An override naming an `:id` that is not in the catalog now throws. Dropping it
silently is the same failure one step along: a typo would leave an operator
reading a config that says the destination is on.

## The provider

`openrouter`, `:openai-compatible`, shipped `:reviewed? false :enabled? false`
like every destination that leaves the machine.

Its default model is **`openrouter/free`**, which is a router over whatever is
free at that moment, not a model. Naming a free model id instead would be the
pinned-name failure this repository measured yesterday, on a faster-moving set:
`stealth/ox-alpha` was the most-used model on OpenRouter and served zero
endpoints four days later.

**No `:assert-response-model?`.** The router answers with the model that served
— measured 2026-08-27, a `openrouter/free` tool-call request returned HTTP 200
with `.model = \"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free\"` and a well-formed call. A
hand-maintained accepted set would refuse that, and refuse a different name
tomorrow, which is exactly ADR-2608270100: the same check against
`murakumo-main` refused 25 of the last 50 runs while the model was generating
fine, and the 24h completion rate read 37.3%.

What that assertion protects elsewhere is `:context-window-tokens`. Here the
bound comes from the other side — the router advertises 200,000 and routes only
to models that can take the request — and the configured budget is 128,000,
deliberately under it rather than at any one model's window.

## What the operator is consenting to

Written into the profile rather than left implied, because the second half is
easy to miss: the transcript goes to OpenRouter, **and onward to whichever third
party serves the free endpoint that minute**, which is a different company on
different turns. Which endpoints are offered at all is decided by the account's
data policy at openrouter.ai/settings/privacy, not by this repository. A
distribution cannot review that for a tenant; this deployment reviews it for
itself.

The fleet stays in the catalog and stays reviewable. It is no longer the
default; it is what an operator picks explicitly.

## Verification

| what | result |
|---|---|
| profile provider override, before | `enabled? false reviewed? false` — silently dropped |
| profile provider override, after | `enabled? true reviewed? true` |
| unknown provider id in a profile | throws `provider override names no configured provider {:unknown [\"olama\"]}` |
| `provider-allowed?` with `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` set | `true`; `select-provider` returns `openrouter` / `openrouter/free` |
| `provider-allowed?` with the key absent | `false`; `select-provider` returns `nil` — fails closed |
| real inference through `provider/chat` | `ITONAMI-FREE-OK`, `usage.cost 0` |
| suite | 1933 tests, 11,464 assertions, 1 failure |

That one failure is `launcher-test/resident-clone-resolves-shell-from-workspace-root`
and is not from this change. It reproduces from bash alone —
`CLOUD_ITONAMI_WORKSPACE_ROOT=<tmp> bin/cloud-itonami-app --print-shell-dir`
prints the real workspace path — and `bin/cloud-itonami-app` contains no
reference to configuration at all. It is a real bug in the launcher's root
resolution and it is left where it was found.

## Not done

- No Bot has completed an unattended workforce turn on the router yet. What is
  measured here is admission and one real completion, not a shift.
- The free tier rate-limits hard. Measured in the superproject the same day,
  nine of fifteen free models answered 429 within one sweep. `provider/chat`
  gets `:max-transient-retries` 0 by default and this entry does not set
  `:model-fallbacks`, so a 429 surfaces as a failed turn rather than falling
  back to the fleet. Whether Bots should walk to `murakumo` on a 429 is a
  routing decision and was not made here.
- The account's OpenRouter data policy was not read programmatically. That free
  endpoints served at all is evidence the account permits them; it is not the
  same as having read the setting.

## 改訂履歴

**2026-08-28, owner instruction: pin a specific free model instead of the
router, because a pinned model performs better than the router's random
draw for this workload.** This reverses the router-not-pinned-name decision
above, on the same measured risk the original decision named (the
`stealth/ox-alpha` four-day disappearance) -- it is not that the risk turned
out to be smaller, it is that it is now bounded two ways it was not before:

1. `data/config.edn`'s `:providers` entry for `openrouter` now sets
   `:default-model \"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free\"` and
   `:model-fallbacks {\"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free\" \"openrouter/free\"}`.
   The pin is this operator's local, gitignored override, per this file's own
   header (\"the store's own file still has the last word\") -- not a change to
   the shipped default in `resources/cloud-itonami-app.defaults.edn`, which
   keeps naming the router. `provider/with-model-fallback` already retries a
   refused request against `:model-fallbacks` in the same turn whenever the
   error type is one of `:provider/timeout`, `:provider/unreachable`,
   `:provider/network-error`, `:provider/http-error`,
   `:provider/model-mismatch`, `:provider/empty-response`, or
   `:provider/model-unready` -- so a pinned model that starts refusing falls
   back to the router within the failing turn, not on the next resident tick.
2. `eaa1334` (`fix/record-requested-model-before-provider-call`, same day)
   closed the gap that would have made this pin's own failures invisible:
   `advance!` only wrote `:provider`/`:requested-model` onto the run AFTER a
   successful response, so every failed turn -- pinned model or router alike
   -- recorded the same unlabeled non-answer. Whichever model is asked now
   survives the call's own failure into the turn record, which is what makes
   \"is the pin still serving\" an answerable, observable question rather
   than one this repository would have to remeasure from scratch the way it
   did for `stealth/ox-alpha`.

The pin was chosen from live data, not a blog ranking: `nvidia/nemotron-3.5-
lightning:free` is verified (2026-08-28, against
`https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models`) to be currently listed, 1M context,
`tools`/`tool_choice` in `:supported_parameters` -- and it is the exact model
this ADR's own \"The provider\" section already measured serving a tool-call
turn on this app. If it disappears from the catalog, the fallback keeps bots
on the router until `data/config.edn` is updated or the pin is removed.

### The bound worked. The risk happened anyway, within about a day.

By the evening of 2026-08-28 the workforce's failure window was 88%
`:provider/fallback-failed` (44 of 50). Direct `curl` against
`https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions` isolated it in three
requests: `nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free` alone, bare
`{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"say hi\"}`, no tools -- **HTTP 404
\"Provider returned error\"**. `openrouter/free` alone, same request --
HTTP 200, served by `nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free`. So the
pinned model was simply gone (`stealth/ox-alpha` again, faster than the
four days that name took), and the router underneath it was fine -- the
`:model-fallbacks` entry was firing exactly as designed, and the fallback
attempt was ALSO failing on most turns, with the identical \"HTTP 400
Backend request failed with status 400\" for every one measured. The
likely reading, not confirmed: the request built for the (now-gone)
pinned model's continuation turn was replayed verbatim against the
fallback rather than rebuilt for whatever the router landed on, and that
whatever also rejected it. This was not investigated further because the
model being gone was sufficient explanation to act on immediately.

**Owner instruction, same day: `minimax/minimax-m3:free`.** Verified live
before pinning, with a direct `curl`, including the specific shape that
had just broken on nemotron -- a tool call, then a continuation turn
replaying the tool result back with its `tool_call_id` -- HTTP 200 both
times.

**The operational lesson, not just the model swap:** a `curl` against a
model directly (bare request, no tools) tells you if the CATALOG entry is
gone. A `curl` reproducing the CONTINUATION shape (tool call, then tool
result replayed back) is what actually caught the fallback's own failure
mode here and is now the standing verification step for any future pin,
named in the header comment above. `data/config.edn`'s own age is not
evidence the pin is still good -- the previous entry read as verified and
current for less than a day."}]
